Node sort(Node head) {
    if (head == null || head.next == null) return head;
    Node mid = split(head);                 // slow/fast -> two halves
    return merge(sort(head), sort(mid));     // recurse + relink merge
}
Node merge(Node a, Node b) {
    Node dummy = new Node(0), tail = dummy;
    while (a != null && b != null) {         // relink, no shifting
        if (a.val <= b.val) { tail.next = a; a = a.next; }
        else                { tail.next = b; b = b.next; }
        tail = tail.next;
    }
    tail.next = (a != null) ? a : b;
    return dummy.next;
}
```
Merging is pure pointer rewiring - exactly what a linked list does cheaply.

```java
record Emp(String name, String dept) {}
List<Emp> list = new ArrayList<>(List.of(
    new Emp("Ann", "Sales"), new Emp("Cara", "Eng"), new Emp("Bob", "Sales")));

// Step 1: sort by NAME (secondary key)
list.sort(Comparator.comparing(Emp::name));
// Step 2: STABLE sort by DEPT (primary key) keeps name order within a dept
list.sort(Comparator.comparing(Emp::dept));
// Result: Eng[Cara], Sales[Ann, Bob]  <- Ann before Bob preserved

// (Comparator.thenComparing does this in one stable step too)
```
`List.sort`/`Collections.sort` use stable TimSort, so the first sort's order survives the second.

